\subsection{Discussion}
The first thing to notice is that average waiting time barely moves: all five strategies land between 94.8 and 95.9\,s. This is not surprising---half the intersections are still under SUMO's actuated control, which dominates the network-wide average. Where the MARL agent pulls ahead is \emph{throughput} and \emph{congestion}. A $+12.0\%$ throughput gain over Fixed-Time means the controlled intersections are clearing vehicles faster, and the $-13.7\%$ drop in congestion index suggests the policy has learned to avoid the kind of gratuitous phase switching that triggers queue spillback.

The composite throughput efficiency metric makes the gap clearest: the MARL agent reaches roughly 1{,}195\,veh/hr per unit congestion, beating Fixed-Time by 29.6\% and MaxPressure by about 3.2\%. It is a useful metric because it penalizes strategies that boost throughput only by increasing congestion elsewhere.

We should be upfront that the Adaptive baseline is a strong contender. Its combination of pressure-aware extension and phase skipping gets it to $+10.1\%$ throughput with very competitive waiting time. The catch is that its parameters were hand-tuned for Cologne; porting it to another city would require re-tuning, whereas the MARL agent learned its policy purely from interaction with the simulator.

