\section{Experiment 2: Elevated Congestion with Reduced Control}
\label{sec:exp2}

\subsection{Setup}
We raised the demand to scale~$=0.85$ and cut the control ratio to 0.2 (16 out of 79 intersections) to see how each strategy copes with less leverage over the network. Six strategies were compared---Fixed-Time, SOTL, Adaptive, MaxPressure, GA, and MARL DQN---over 30 episodes, all sharing the same base seed (500) so that every episode presents identical traffic to every controller.

The MARL checkpoint used here is \textbf{v16} (\texttt{marl\_cologne\_\ldots\_v16\_demand085\_di10.pt}), trained with \(K{=}12\) lane padding and a reduced switch penalty (\(c_{\text{switch}}{=}0.01\)). Crucially, the MARL agent uses a decision interval of \(\Delta t{=}10\)\,s, while all five baselines use \(\Delta t{=}5\)\,s. We chose the longer step for MARL after analysing how the control interval interacts with the signal program (Section~\ref{sec:yellow_overhead}): with \(\Delta t{=}5\)\,s, roughly 3\,s of yellow clearance after a phase change occupies about 60\% of each decision window, during which competing approaches are not discharged, which made learning a useful switching policy difficult. Setting \(\Delta t{=}10\)\,s reduces yellow to about 30\% of the window. We acknowledge that this creates an asymmetry in the comparison; interpreting the results below should keep this difference in mind.

