\subsection{Discussion}
The most striking feature of Table~\ref{tab:exp2_results} is how compressed the results are: the entire spread in waiting time is under 1\,s. Two factors drive this. First, only 16 of 79 intersections are controlled, so no strategy has much leverage over the network average. Second, at scale~$0.85$ the network is near saturation---there is simply less room for any controller to make a difference.

Within that narrow band the MARL agent places second (100.11\,s), trailing Adaptive by just 0.11\,s and beating MaxPressure, GA, Fixed-Time, and SOTL. It is worth remembering that v16 was \emph{trained} at ratio~$=0.5$ but \emph{evaluated} at ratio~$=0.2$; the fact that the policy does not collapse under this distribution shift is encouraging, even if the absolute improvement is small.

Emissions tell a similar story. Adaptive has the lowest total CO$_2$ ($7.110 \times 10^9$\,mg), MARL is right behind ($7.114 \times 10^9$\,mg), and the full spread across all six strategies is less than 0.2\%. At this congestion level, shared traffic volume and vehicle kinematics dominate total emissions far more than signal timing does.

GA finishes fourth in waiting time---its offline parameter search cannot adapt once traffic shifts within an episode, which limits it compared to the reactive approaches.

\subsubsection{Decision interval and yellow-phase overhead}
\label{sec:yellow_overhead}
Early Experiment~2 runs with \(\Delta t{=}5\)\,s produced a policy that seldom changed phase. Log inspection showed that each switch invokes roughly 3\,s of yellow, i.e.\ about 60\% of a 5\,s window, with no release of queued traffic to green during that interval. Under the reward structure used, the agent rationally treated switching as rarely beneficial and converged toward minimal phase changes.

We addressed this by setting \(\Delta t{=}10\)\,s (yellow then spans \(\sim\)30\% of the window) and reducing the switch penalty from 0.05 to 0.01. The resulting v16 agent switches about 15\% of the time, compared with about 11\% under the earlier configuration. It still favours holding green, but it applies phase changes when queue pressure warrants them.

