\section{Training Dynamics}
\label{sec:training}

This section looks under the hood at how the MARL agent learns its policy, based on the training logs from the v16 run (300 episodes on Cologne).

\subsection{Loss Convergence}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/report_figure_pdfs/training_loss_curve.pdf}
    \caption{Training loss (Smooth L1) over episodes.}
    \label{fig:loss_curve}
\end{figure}

The loss curve (Figure~\ref{fig:loss_curve}) drops steadily but never reaches zero. That residual is expected: each episode uses a fresh SUMO seed, so the agent faces a different traffic scenario every time and cannot memorize a fixed mapping.

\subsection{Switch Rate Evolution}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/report_figure_pdfs/training_switch_rate_curve.pdf}
    \caption{Phase switch rate over training episodes. The decline from $\sim$0.25 to $\sim$0.15 reflects the agent learning that frequent switching is costly.}
    \label{fig:switch_rate}
\end{figure}

Early on, with \(\epsilon\) still high, the agent switches about 25\% of the time. As it learns, that drops to roughly 15\% (Figure~\ref{fig:switch_rate}). The fact that it does not converge to zero tells us something useful: the agent has identified specific traffic states where switching is worth the yellow-time cost, rather than blindly holding every phase.

\subsection{Exploration Schedule}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/report_figure_pdfs/training_epsilon_decay.pdf}
    \caption{Exploration rate ($\epsilon$) decay over training episodes.}
    \label{fig:epsilon}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:epsilon} shows the \(\epsilon\) decay from 1.0 to 0.05. The small upward spikes are the plateau recovery mechanism kicking in---when the moving-average reward flatlines, we bump \(\epsilon\) to force re-exploration. In at least two of our training runs this appeared to help the agent escape a local optimum around episode~150.

\subsection{Convergence Analysis}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/report_figure_pdfs/training_convergence_analysis.pdf}
    \caption{Convergence analysis: loss, per-traffic-light reward, and \(\epsilon\) overlaid.}
    \label{fig:convergence}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:convergence} overlays loss, per-traffic-light reward, and \(\epsilon\). The steepest loss drop happens in the first 100 episodes, which is exactly when the curriculum is ramping the controlled-intersection count from 25\% to 50\%. Once the curriculum finishes the loss still falls, but more slowly---the agent is fine-tuning rather than restructuring its policy.

\subsection{Curriculum Learning and Plateau Recovery}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/report_figure_pdfs/training_curriculum_plateau.pdf}
    \caption{Curriculum learning and plateau recovery dynamics (v16, 300 episodes). (a)~Controlled-intersection ratio ramps linearly from 25\% to 50\% over the first 100 episodes. (b)~Per-traffic-light reward moving average (MA-20); orange dashed lines mark episodes where plateau recovery triggered. (c)~Exploration rate~\(\epsilon\) showing the overall linear decay punctuated by temporary upward bumps each time the plateau detector fires.}
    \label{fig:curriculum_plateau}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:curriculum_plateau} makes the two mechanisms visually explicit. Panel~(a) shows the curriculum ramp: the agent starts with roughly 10 intersections and gains one or two more every few episodes until it reaches the target of 40 at episode~100. This keeps the early gradient signal manageable. Panel~(b) confirms that the reward drops most sharply during the ramp (the agent is constantly adapting to a growing workload) and then stabilizes once the full set is active. The orange markers in panels~(b) and~(c) correspond to plateau-recovery events---moments when the moving-average reward stagnated for 15~consecutive episodes, triggering a learning-rate decay and a temporary \(\epsilon\) boost. The sawtooth pattern in panel~(c) around episodes~50--300 shows these bumps clearly. In practice, the early plateau events (episodes~40--100) coincide with curriculum transitions, while the later ones (episodes~150+) reflect genuine policy stagnation that the recovery mechanism helps the agent escape.

\subsection{Interpretation}
Taken together, these curves tell a consistent story. The agent converges on a \emph{conservative} policy: it learns that yellow-time and queue disruption usually outweigh the benefit of switching to a nominally better phase. In plain terms, its strategy is ``hold unless the pressure differential clearly justifies the cost of switching.'' We initially worried this looked like a failure to learn, but the evaluation results in Sections~\ref{sec:exp1} and~\ref{sec:exp2} show that it is actually a rational strategy---one that delivers the best throughput and congestion numbers in Experiment~1 and remains competitive in Experiment~2.

