\subsection{Experiment 2: Elevated Congestion}

Experiment~2 shows the same pattern but even more compressed: total CO$_2$ ranges from $7.110$ to $7.122 \times 10^9$\,mg across all six strategies, a spread of less than 0.2\%. NO$_x$ and PM$_x$ are tighter still, within 0.1\%.

The takeaway is straightforward: when only 20\% of intersections are controlled, signal timing has almost no effect on total emissions. The dominant factors are aggregate traffic volume and the speed--acceleration profiles dictated by the car-following model. Signal control mostly determines \emph{where} vehicles idle, not \emph{how much} they emit in total. This is consistent with SUMO's HBEFA model, which is driven by instantaneous speed and acceleration rather than cumulative waiting time. Figure~\ref{fig:co2_detail} further illustrates the per-episode CO$_2$ overlap in Experiment~1.

