\section{Summary of Results}
\label{sec:results_summary}

To pull the threads together:

\begin{itemize}
    \item In Experiment~1 (moderate congestion, ratio~0.5) the MARL agent (v8) leads on throughput ($+12.0\%$), congestion index ($-13.7\%$) and throughput efficiency ($+29.6\%$), while its waiting time is within 1.1\,s of the best baseline. These comparisons are unpaired (different seeds); the large sample size (120 episodes) provides reasonable statistical confidence.

    \item In Experiment~2 (elevated congestion, ratio~0.2) the MARL agent (v16) finishes second in waiting time---0.11\,s behind Adaptive---despite controlling fewer intersections than it was trained on and using a longer decision interval (\(\Delta t{=}10\)\,s vs 5\,s for the baselines). These comparisons are paired (shared seed 500).

    \item Parameter sharing lets a single set of weights control anywhere from 16 to 40 intersections with no architecture change. Inference cost is the same as one forward pass of a small MLP.

    \item The agent learns a conservative switching policy. It only changes phases when the pressure gap justifies the yellow-time penalty---an emergent behaviour, not a training artefact.

    \item Emissions barely differ across strategies ($<0.2\%$ in both experiments). The one interesting metric is per-arrived-vehicle CO$_2$ in Experiment~1, where the MARL agent and MaxPressure both achieve $-10.7\%$ vs Fixed-Time, simply because higher throughput spreads the fixed emission load over more completed trips.

    \item The Adaptive controller deserves credit as a strong hand-tuned baseline. It consistently places first or second, but it required manual parameter tuning that would need to be repeated for every new network.
\end{itemize}

Overall, the results show that a parameter-sharing MARL approach is competitive with---and in several metrics superior to---established baselines on a city-scale OSM network. The advantages are clearest in throughput and congestion management rather than in raw waiting time.
